Hand in hand, we gaze through tear-blurred eyes;  
speech fails, and we choke on silence.  
I think of the road stretching on and on—  
a thousand miles of misty waters;  
heavy dusk spreads beneath the vast Chu sky.

Holding hands, we gaze, our eyes brimming with tears;
At last, no words—choked with sobs.
Thinking of the journey onward, a thousand miles of misty waters;
The evening haze sinks deep beneath the vast Chu sky.

Heed not the sound of wind piercing the woods and striking the leaves;
Why not chant and whistle, and walk on at an unhurried pace?
A bamboo staff and straw sandals—lighter than riding horseback;
Who’s afraid? In a straw rain cape, I’ll let mist and rain shape my life.

Rain slants, wild winds rage at March’s end;
At dusk I close the door—no way to keep spring here.
Through tearful eyes I ask the flowers;
They answer not. Fallen blossoms whirl past the swing.

